# Explore the Mathematics of Self-Modifying Computation

Begin with the operational surprise: changing code can be represented as changing data. The paired traces below let you see this equivalence one transition at a time.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{algorithm:0}}

{{demo:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the simulation argument</summary>Store the current program and ordinary state as one enlarged state. Both systems inspect the same pair, halt in the same case, and otherwise produce the same successor pair. Induction on the number of transitions gives equality of every finite run.</details>

## Bounded evidence is one-sided

A bounded monitor can discover a finite halting witness, but “not observed” cannot be promoted to “never.” Explore that distinction interactively, then compare several witness stages in the visualization. For background, see the [Halting problem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Halting_problem) and [computability theory](https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/computability/).

{{interactive_demo:0}}

{{algorithm:1}}

{{visualization:0}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the diagonal fixed-point obstruction</summary>If every Boolean predicate could be represented by a row of a universal table, complement the diagonal entry of each row. The resulting predicate differs from row $i$ at argument $i$, so it is absent from the table. This is the Boolean form of [Cantor's theorem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cantor%27s_theorem) and the logical core of universal halting undecidability.</details>

## Finite observers must merge histories

An alphabet with at least one symbol generates infinitely many finite streams. A finite memory has only finitely many outputs, so collisions are unavoidable. Targeted forgetting makes those collisions visible.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{algorithm:2}}

{{visualization:1}}

{{visualization:2}}

{{demo:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the quotient theorem</summary>Declare $u\sim v$ exactly when the memory values agree. The assignment $[u]\mapsto m(u)$ is well defined, multiplicative, injective by the definition of $\sim$, and surjective onto the image. Thus observable memory is precisely the quotient of streams by indistinguishability. For context, see [quotient algebra](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quotient_algebra) and [free monoids](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Free_monoid).</details>

## Where to go next

The practical lesson is not to stop analyzing programs. It is to state restrictions explicitly: bounded resources, finite-state fragments, conservative abstractions, or selected observables. Open the expert sections above to connect each experiment with its theorem.

